		body: "<task>\nPhase F1 builds the shared contract every later core uses. Repo: STACK at /workspace.\n\nRead first: docs/spec/Stella Feature Coverage and Extensibility Plan.md (sections 2, 6, 8, 10b, 12); docs/spec/Pre-Stella Roadmap.md (Gate 0, Phase 0.5); docs/spec/Post-GoLive Provider Flexibility Audit Prompt.md; docs/spec/Stella App Post-GoLive.md (\"AI provider compatibility\", \"Provider and integration flexibility\", \"Per-project provider, effort and model overrides\", \"Extensibility and supportability\"); docs/spec/Stella FreeLLMAPI Provider Candidate.md.\n\nPreflight. Check Gate 0 and the Stage 5/6 freeze, and record both in progress.md and at the top of the report: the evidence that Gate 0 passed (the go-live record and its commit) and that the freeze is lifted for every file this phase changes. If the freeze is still on for files this phase must change, stop and report which files and why. That is a deliberate blocker.\n\nPart A, read-only audit (write only to .scratch/F1/audit.md). Establish from the live system, not from the docs:\n- The engine (Hermes) version, and whether a profile can run on any provider: local server, OpenAI-compatible endpoint, Anthropic API, Gemini API. Custom endpoints, tool calling, streaming, subagents, skills, MCP.\n- Each coding CLI that is installed (OpenCode, Claude Code, Codex CLI, Gemini CLI, others): non-interactive mode, model selection, permission config format, MCP support, API key vs subscription login.\n- Where every credential lives today, how the network allowlist works, and every role in roles.json with its tools.\n- How agents find tools and skills today, and what exists: skills folders, MCP servers, bin/ commands.\n- Every hard-coded literal (start from section 12 of the Plan).\nEnd the audit with a \"what would break\" list: for each later phase (F2, F3, broker, engine, VM, harness), what in today's stack would block it.\n\nPart B, contract (docs, schemas, validators, tests; no new services):\n1. Connections catalog: providers, apps and channels as one catalog. Fields: id, kind, hosts, auth types, actions with approval class, models with probed capabilities (tool calling, JSON mode, context size, vision, streaming), cost, limits, data-policy flags (retention, training use, region, zero-retention available), terms-of-service flags.\n2. Role requirements: extend roles.json (do not replace it) so each role states what a model needs to hold it: tool calling, minimum context, vision, allowed data classes. Add rows for tester, validator, verifier, red team, router, voice, look and proposer if missing, and for the build roster's checker, critical-verifier and critical-red-team, so F2's build-team preset validates.\n3. Role assignments: role-assignments data. Scope (global, project, card), role, an ordered chain of {provider, model, parameters}, per effort mode. Precedence: data policy, then card, then project, then global. An override can never widen the data policy.\n4. Capability registry: one schema for tools, skills, MCP servers, commands and extensions: id, kind, summary, keywords, inputs, outputs, allowed roles, risk class, hosts, secrets, status (proposed, approved, disabled), source, triage result.\n5. features.json, the extension manifest, the event-log schema, the channel-neutral approval-service interface, versions.json with tested-with fields, and the settings registry from section 10b.\n6. Owner-only write path: owner settings (product rule 3) become protected paths for every role. A selftest tries each role (write tool, shell redirect, git, delegated subagent) and expects a refusal. This item is tagged critical.\n7. Hard-code lint in npm test, with the section 12 literals as its baseline allow-list. Fix the cheap ones now: profile names in merge-card.sh and escalation-thread.sh (look up by role), constants in watchdog.sh and escalate.sh (settings data).\n8. Apply contract (Plan section 2, item 8, and section 10b). No prompt before this one builds it, and only parts exist today (apply-hermes-local.sh has --check). Build one generic apply command for every data file and setting: --dry-run shows the diff and changes nothing; it takes a backup; --check reports drift from the committed or last-applied state; apply is idempotent; rollback restores the backup; every apply and rollback writes one event-log line (who, what, old and new value, secrets masked). Reuse the --check pattern of apply-hermes-local.sh, and say in the audit what else already exists. The owner-only write path in item 6 uses it. This item is tagged critical.\n</task>\n\n<acceptance>\n- audit.md exists, answers every Part A question from live evidence, and ends with the \"what would break\" list.\n- Every schema validates. A fake provider, fake app, fake channel, fake role, fake capability and fake extension each validate and load with no code change.\n- The lint passes on the baseline and fails on a planted literal provider, model, host and board name.\n- Every role is refused when it writes each owner setting. The owner path writes one through the apply contract, and rollback restores it.\n- Apply contract: a dry run changes no file; apply then rollback leaves the files byte-identical to before; a second apply is a no-op; --check reports a hand edit as drift; each apply and rollback leaves one event-log line with no secret value in it.\n- Behavior of the live stack is unchanged apart from the protected paths and the cheap fixes. The existing selftests still pass.\n</acceptance>",
