		id: "V3",
		title: "real-hardware verification",
		body: "<task>\nPhase V3 proves the VM system on real machines. Repo: STACK at /workspace.\n\nRead first: .scratch/V1/REPORT.md, .scratch/V2/REPORT.md, .scratch/E3/REPORT.md.\n\n1. Write one runbook per OS the owner has: Windows (WHPX; note whether the edition is Home or Pro), Linux (KVM), macOS (HVF, plus the Virtualization.framework backend on Apple Silicon) if available. Each runbook boots the engine through its adapter, creates a Linux build VM and one test VM as a sibling of the engine, runs the V2 smoke card in each, destroys them, and runs the orphan check. From each guest it also tries 10.0.2.2 on each loopback port the host uses, a LAN address, an unapproved host and a DNS-rebinding name; all must fail. In each guest OS it also runs the accessibility-tree check (open an app, fill a field, save, verify by re-reading; a blind app falls back to screen and vision), records which test apps expose a usable tree and which controls lack bounds, and, on Windows, checks whether the vendored binary trips the guest's security controls (unsigned binaries can). Each step has the exact command, the expected output, and the evidence to paste back.\n2. Extend the E3 matrix script with the VM checks, so one run per machine gives one JSON result.\n3. Write the guest licence notes into the templates catalog: Windows guest images and their terms, macOS guests only on Apple hardware. The defaults are the 2026-11-13 set (V1 item 3); the owner ticks which OS families to install and may change versions. If the owner switched on the OSX-KVM fallback, the runbook boots it and records it as proven or unproven.\n4. List what nested virtualization would change, as a recorded fallback only, not built. Record that QEMU's security policy names KVM and HVF but not WHPX, so isolation under WHPX is unverified upstream, and that software emulation proves function, never isolation.\n5. Per OS, measure cold boot, saved-state resume and warm-pool start times; check that reseed gives each clone a new identity; and for Windows guests, record what the graphics can run.\n6. Build the optional 3D backend V1 designed (owner decision 2026-11-11), picked from what the owner's Windows machine has (for example a hypervisor with a 3D virtual GPU that is free for personal use; check its licence and pin its version), behind the vm-driver interface and used only for templates that ask for 3D. If no candidate works, record it as unproven; QEMU stays the default either way.\n7. Prepare the Blender templates A1 uses: a Linux VM template with a pinned Blender for headless work, and the 3D-backend template when that backend works.\n</task>\n\n<acceptance>\n- One runbook per OS, each ending with a single pasteable JSON result, and every guest egress check in it fails as expected. Each records the accessibility-tree check result for that guest OS.\n- The report records cold boot, resume and warm-pool times per OS, the 3D backend as built or unproven, and the Blender templates as ready for A1.\n- The matrix script runs here and marks the other OSes \"not run\".\n- After the owner pastes results, the fix-round prompt turns each mismatch into a finding.\n</acceptance>",
